x265: restore vxrm after the RVV filter intrinsics that change it

filter-prim.cpp's vert_sp and vert_ss intrinsics narrow with
__riscv_vnclip_wv_i16m1(..., 2, vl), i.e. vxrm = 2 (round-down). The compiler
writes vxrm before the instruction and never puts it back. The assembly kernels
(dct.S and others) use vnclip.wi without setting vxrm and rely on the process
default of 0 (round-to-nearest-up), so any thread that has run one of these
filters goes on to round differently in the next DCT.

Effect on an 8-bit RVV build: the bitstream differs from the C reference, and
because vxrm is per hart it also depends on thread scheduling, so two runs of the
same command on the same machine can produce different files.

This restores vxrm = 0 on return from the two public wrappers. It does not touch
the 20 call sites, and the kernels in intrapred.S / mc-a.S already set vxrm
themselves. A fix that makes the assembly set vxrm on entry would be sturdier
and is for upstream to decide. HIGH_BIT_DEPTH builds do not compile these filters.

Applies to x265 4.2 and to b81f650 (4.2 + 3). x265 4.1 has no riscv64 code.
See KNOWN-ISSUES-x265-rvv-vxrm.md in the internal notes for the investigation.

--- a/source/common/riscv64/filter-prim.cpp	2026-10-01 19:00:46.174489273 +0800
+++ b/source/common/riscv64/filter-prim.cpp	2026-10-02 01:01:41.271852485 +0800
@@ -936,7 +936,7 @@
 }
 
 template<int N, int width, int height>
-void interp_vert_sp_rvv(const int16_t *src, intptr_t srcStride, pixel *dst,
+void interp_vert_sp_rvv_impl(const int16_t *src, intptr_t srcStride, pixel *dst,
                          intptr_t dstStride, int coeffIdx)
 {
     if (N == 8)
@@ -968,6 +968,16 @@
     }
 }
 
+/* The intrinsics above use vxrm=2 and the compiler does not restore it, but the
+ * assembly kernels (dct.S, ...) rely on the default vxrm=0. */
+template<int N, int width, int height>
+void interp_vert_sp_rvv(const int16_t *src, intptr_t srcStride, pixel *dst,
+                         intptr_t dstStride, int coeffIdx)
+{
+    interp_vert_sp_rvv_impl<N, width, height>(src, srcStride, dst, dstStride, coeffIdx);
+    asm volatile("csrwi vxrm, 0" ::: "memory");
+}
